docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.eli16.md:15:**Extra lock on the three riskiest switches.** Three of the allowed settings can turn OFF a detector that watches the system itself (like the token-burn alarm). Those get a stronger rule: I can't flip them off on my own at all — the server itself messages you for a yes/no, and only YOUR reply commits the change. Turning a detector back ON needs no such friction. And every "detector off" decision posts one calm, un-deletable notice to your alerts topic — so nothing can ever be quieted invisibly, not even by me.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.eli16.md:20:- **Machines already running** can't absorb most of these settings mid-flight (they read settings at startup), so they mark themselves "will apply on next restart" — visible on the dashboard — and quietly restart at a clean moment (never while handling your messages or mid-work). That's the "auto-apply" you approved.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.eli16.md:21:- **Undo is one lever:** delete the notebook entry and every machine returns to its own local file value — including machines that are offline right now (the deletion syncs like everything else, so nothing "resurrects" later).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.eli16.md:27:- If the sync layer is down, machines just keep their current local settings — nothing breaks, and the status page shows sync state.
docs/specs/context-wedge-detection-completeness.eli16.md:5:The failure is simpler: the monitor sees only the latest terminal lines. It can see the real banner on one poll, then lose sight of it after more output scrolls the banner upward. Losing the visible banner currently also loses the fact that the trusted detector already matched it.
tests/e2e/sync-orchestrator-lifecycle.test.ts:5: * to user-visible outcome works end-to-end, with controlled (but real)
docs/specs/context-wedge-detection-completeness.md:60:`SessionRecovery` remains the sole authority. A remembered boolean reaches the same context-recovery branch, which still applies every existing guard and bound. Any non-success recovery result leaves the boolean set and schedules nothing; later presentation happens only through the monitor's already-existing poll and cooldown.
docs/specs/context-wedge-detection-completeness.md:84:Inside `checkAndRecover`, current detector output remains preferred for the existing matched-pattern message. When no current pattern is visible but the boolean is true, the same context-recovery method runs without inventing a replacement pattern identity.
docs/specs/context-wedge-detection-completeness.md:86:The exact latched-only call is `recoverFromContextExhaustion(topicId, sessionName, null)`. `null` affects message rendering only: the existing method omits its optional current-pattern clause. Cooldown keys remain the existing `context:${sessionName}` key and branch selection remains the existing context-recovery branch. No log or user message claims that the pattern is currently visible.
docs/specs/SESSION-ACTIVITY-VERDICT-SPEC.eli16.md:5:The user-visible result: you send a message, get told "received, actively working on it," later get told it was never delivered, while a watchdog simultaneously declares the same session stuck. Three contradictory stories about one session. Internally it's just as bad — every fix to one component's heuristic leaves the other four wrong.
docs/specs/SESSION-ACTIVITY-VERDICT-SPEC.eli16.md:9:Then, one consumer at a time, the five components switch from their homemade heuristics to reading the shared verdict. The age-limit check stops deferring forever on idle sessions. Updates stop being blocked by sessions that aren't doing anything. The user-facing "actively working" is only said when the session is actually working — and when it's stalled, the wording says that honestly. Importantly, nothing about KILLING sessions changes here: each component keeps its own authority over what to do; this only unifies the facts they act on.
docs/specs/SESSION-ACTIVITY-VERDICT-SPEC.eli16.md:13:After adversarial review (Codey, on the PR), three things got tightened. First, the answer is computed fresh but remembered for five seconds — and everyone asking within those five seconds gets the SAME measurement, because one of the inputs (CPU progress since last look) is consumed by the act of looking; two components measuring independently would corrupt each other's readings. Anything making a destructive decision can refuse a stale answer instead of trusting it. Second, "unknown" now has a written contract per consumer (the reaper keeps, updates defer, the user-facing voice stops claiming "actively working") so it can't quietly turn back into five private guesses. Third, for session types whose idle-prompt shape we haven't characterized (codex, gemini), the system is explicitly forbidden from concluding "idle" — measurements can prove a session is working or stalled, but never that it's safely idle, so nothing destructive fires on a guess.
