docs/specs/worktree-git-hooks-seed.eli16.md:22:- **`seedGitHooks`** — a helper that copies the hooks folder into a fresh checkout. What it is NOT allowed to do: it never blocks or slows down making a worktree. If anything is uncertain — no hooks configured, the path looks suspicious, nothing to copy — it quietly does nothing and moves on. It fails toward "let the worktree get created," never toward "stop."
docs/specs/worktree-git-hooks-seed.eli16.md:30:**Does nothing when there's nothing to do.** Repos that don't use this hooks style, or already have the folder, are left exactly as they were — a genuine no-op.
docs/specs/account-machine-matrix.md:34:Clicking "Set up" runs the whole auth IN THE DASHBOARD: it starts a fresh sign-in on the target machine (that machine re-mints its own login — Mechanism B, ToS-safe, nothing copied), opens the provider auth page, and the cell turns into a **code input + Submit**. The operator authorizes, gets the provider's code, pastes it into the cell, taps Submit; the code rides to the target machine, completes the login, and the cell flips to **✓**. At-a-glance "who's active where," and the auth never leaves the dashboard.
docs/specs/account-machine-matrix.md:41:- **`POST /subscription-pool/matrix/start-cell`** `{ accountId, machineId, pin }` (dev-gated): **requires the dashboard PIN** (`checkMandatePin`), exactly like `/mandate/issue-for-machine`. With a valid PIN it does, server-side: resolve the agents pair + the account's email from pool state (NEVER from the body — the same `resolveFollowMeEnrollTarget` path `enroll/start` uses), issue the per-`(account, target-machine)` mandate via the existing PIN-gated issuance, then drive the existing mandate-gated `enroll/start` (delivered-mandate path for a peer target — the proven cross-machine flow that enrolled adriana). Returns `{ verificationUrl, loginId, machineId }`. Audited: `{accountId, machineId, outcome}`, never a code or token. Partial-failure semantics (codex r3 #3): idempotent — if mandate issuance succeeds but enroll-start fails/times out, start-cell returns an honest retryable error and the unused mandate lapses by its existing TTL (an unconsumed mandate enrolls nothing — harmless + bounded); re-tapping first CHECKS for an existing valid pending login for that `(accountId, machineId)` (correct expectedEmail + target + un-expired) — if present it is REUSED (its `verificationUrl` returned, no new mandate minted); only if there is none does it mint a fresh scoped mandate + enroll-start. So a retry never duplicates a pending login or stacks mandates. Why PIN, not Bearer (the load-bearing correction): the AGENT also holds the Bearer token (skills/scripts call the local API with it), so Bearer-only would let any agent session start a cross-machine account login with no operator present — re-creating the exact hole the PIN gate on `issue-for-machine` exists to close (Know Your Principal). The PIN is the only thing in this system that proves operator presence; the matrix tap collects it inline (Operators-act-in-taps). start-cell adds NO new authority — it is a UI-convenience wrapper that drives the existing PIN→mandate→enroll-start chain in one call so the frontend never handles fingerprints or raw mandate JSON.
docs/specs/account-machine-matrix.md:54:- **FD7 — Cell pivot key + integrity.** The grid pivots pool-scope rows on `(accountId, machineId)` (NOT `accountId` — the same account on two machines is the normal, individually-visible case). Cells are computed ONLY after separating reachable machines from the pool-scope `failed`/stale set; an account row from a peer that went dark is rendered under that machine's offline column, never as a confident ✓. Tested both ways (offline peer column vs genuinely-empty cell).
