docs/specs/ws52-code-paste-back.md:97:- **FD16 — Prompt detection is a deliberate heuristic, not a brittle exact-match (codex r4 #1).** The readiness check requires both "paste" and "code" in the captured frame rather than an exact prompt string (`Paste code here if prompted >`). This is intentional per Signal-vs-Authority: the CLI prompt wording varies across versions (the same variance that caused the URL line-wrap fix), so a brittle exact-match would false-NEGATIVE and strand valid codes. The realistic failure mode the check defends against — the login process EXITED and the pane dropped to a shell — never shows both words, so the heuristic is adequate; it sits on top of has-session (it is OUR enroll pane, not an arbitrary shell). The accepted residual: a frame whose scrollback happens to contain both words could pass; the downside is bounded (the code is single-use and goes to our own login pane). Mitigated further by capturing only the last ~12 lines (near the live prompt, codex r5 #1) AND a NEGATIVE shell-prompt check (codex r6 #1): the last visible line must NOT end in a shell sigil (`$`/`%`/`#`) — an exited login drops to a shell prompt ending in one of those, while the real Claude prompt ends in `>`. Both the positive (paste+code present) and negative (not-a-shell) checks must hold before a single char is typed. A PTY-state wrapper that records an explicit `waiting_for_code` was considered and rejected as a large rebuild disproportionate to a screen-scrape that already gates on has-session + positive + negative checks.
