docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass22-verdict.md:142:**The decline is real, it is now six readings deep, and it is not an artifact of grading.** Against a seventeen-pass plateau of 4–6. Third consecutive pass with no defect in the four registry guards, attacked with shapes nobody has tried. All four claimed sabotages reproduce to the exact test. The archive limb is, for the first time, verified as *enforced*. The populations self-extend by input in both directions I tested. And the author's self-reference discipline held under adversarial test: both arming loops were resolved by rewording prose, and re-introducing either still arms the guard, so nothing was narrowed to buy quiet.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass22-verdict.md:162:The machinery is in the best state of the twenty-two readings. Every arm I attacked refused exactly its own input and nothing else. Four author sabotages and four of mine each reddened precisely the tests they should. The four registry guards are untouched and clean under fresh probes. The 47-step chain is green. The archive is complete, contiguous, filed alone ten minutes before the repair it prompted, and — verified, not asserted — defended by a limb that refuses.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass22-verdict.md:170:**reject.** This is the strongest state the branch has been in and that belongs before the refusal: three consecutive passes with no defect in the four registry guards, this time under probe shapes nobody had tried; the archive limb verified as *machinery* for the first time in the series — I removed the verdict and watched the build refuse rather than reading that it would; the derivations self-extending by input in both directions, including past the byte boundary the last reading found; both self-inflicted arming loops resolved by rewording prose rather than narrowing a population, which I confirmed by re-introducing them; all four claimed sabotages reproducing to the exact test in a clean clone; the 47-step chain and the 41-test suite green; and on the one contested number from last cycle, pass 21's "47 steps" is right and I confirmed it. Magnitude is genuinely declining and I credit that clause without qualification, for the fourth consecutive pass. It fails the other clause as the last five passes did. The tens table added this cycle was justified in writing by the exact property it does not have and cannot resolve the ordinal for the thirtieth reading, which is this document's own heading convention; the repair for "you over-state the derived population" published a count of that population which the guard contradicts on every run; the repair for "your clean-case control is vacuous" produced a control that cannot fail; the two sentences the last reading named as false about wrap handling are unchanged while the commit added a surface where they are more false than before; and two of the four citation forms this commit announces have no test at all, so nothing here can fail when they break — which is the absence that started this streak. The next move is small and it is not another population: finish the ordinal table at the round numbers, make the scan strip a leading comment or quote marker before joining, re-derive the two counts in the header from the guard rather than from a reviewer's verdict, give `ACCEPTS a cited review pass` a pass number the fixture does not already cite, and add a sabotage for the plural and hash forms — then correct the two wrap sentences to the rule the scan implements. After that the remainder really is dated, named work.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:47:- **Record identity:** `recordKey` = the canonical config key path. One record per key (the overlay is the union of records). **Multi-key quieting is intentionally non-atomic** — each key is an independent record; a partially-landed group is visible per-key on the census. No batch semantics in v1. (A bundle/"desired profile" record shape was considered and rejected: it adds a second record kind with its own partial-application semantics, while per-key LWW + per-key census visibility already surfaces exactly which pieces landed. The conversational layer MUST warn when one operator intent maps to multiple independent writes — "this quiets 3 settings; they apply independently.")
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:49:- **Audit trail:** every ACCEPTED write is an append-only journal envelope — LWW keeps one *live* record per key, but the journal history retains the full write stream (who/when/what, including churn). The census row additionally carries `writeCount24h` per key so burst-churn is visible at a glance, and the write funnel enforces ONE counter: the **per-key write-attempt cap of 6 per 24h** — incremented by every request that passes validation, i.e. commits AND uncommitted suppression 202s alike; refused requests (400/409) do not count; excess ⇒ 429 naming the cap. Churn cannot hide inside LWW coalescing.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:52:- **Bounds:** retention 256 KiB (steady state ≤ 17 records, ~30× headroom for churn history), rate-cap 20 capacity / 5 refill-per-sec on the local route (429 + retry-after; nothing queues silently). **The rate-cap governs LOCAL writes only** — replicated receives bypass it (they were rate-bounded at their origin; dropping receives would lose data).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:84:   - **Reply matching (closed grammar, no interpretation):** a commit requires an authenticated inbound message from the operator that is EITHER a Telegram reply-to the server's own prompt message containing the bare case-insensitive token `yes`/`no`, OR a message containing `yes <pendingId>`/`no <pendingId>` (this second form exists precisely so confirmation never DEPENDS on reply-to metadata surviving a client/bridge — it works from any client as a plain message). Anything else matches nothing — ONE format reminder per pending (not per message), then silence until match or expiry. Only the FIRST match on a pending is acted on; message EDITS are never re-processed for matching (an edited "no"→"yes" cannot reverse a decision). Ambiguous natural language is deliberately NOT interpreted — a non-matching reply can never commit.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:85:   - **Principal re-verified at reply time:** the pending captures the verified operator principal at POST; the commit requires the replying sender to be authenticated, to MATCH the captured principal, AND to still be the topic's currently-bound verified operator. An operator-binding change between POST and reply VOIDS the pending (visible as `voided: operator-changed`).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:86:   - **Expiry 15 minutes.** Pendings persist in the local store (survive restart); expiry is evaluated on the watcher tick and swept at boot. On expiry or decline: nothing commits, and ONE outcome line is sent to the initiating topic ("that quiet-settings change wasn't confirmed in time — nothing was changed; re-request if still wanted"), so a not-landed change is never silent. Expired/declined/voided pendings remain census-visible with their status for 24h, then age out. Re-request is allowed immediately (subject to the rate/churn caps).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:90:2. **Non-recallable notice:** at commit time, one calm notice line is DELIVERED to the Attention hub ("burn-detection alerts were turned off via quiet-settings by <operator> — Nth change to this key today — census link"). Delivery is the hub's Telegram send at raise time; a later agent-side attention resolution cannot recall a delivered message. Dedupe: one notice per (keyPath, suppressing-value, setBy) per 24h rolling window, persisted in store metadata (survives restarts); the census `writeCount24h` keeps repeat churn visible even when the notice dedupes. Detector display names derive from the keyPath's second segment, kebab-cased (`burnDetection` → "burn-detection").
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:114:- **First-rejoin honesty (the ancestor incident, precisely scoped):** a machine that was OFFLINE when a quiet-record was created boots BEFORE that record has synced — so its first minutes can still run the old (loud) values: the incident is reduced from a multi-day durable re-flood to a bounded transient window (journal sync lands the record within the first sync pass; then one restart applies it). Two things bound that window: (a) **rejoin fast-path** — a `pending-restart` created within the first 10 minutes after boot is treated as boot-continuation: no dwell, restart at the FIRST clean window (a just-booted machine reaches one almost immediately); (b) independently, the #1456 calm-alerting layer already suppresses routine rejoin/update-window alarms, which is exactly the class the loud window would emit. Deliberately NOT chosen: blocking boot on a journal catch-up (re-introduces the network-wait race the boot invariant exists to kill). **The guarantee, phrased exactly: quiet is EVENTUAL — after the first successful sync plus one clean-window restart** (normal case: minutes). Sync delayed-but-not-wedged is not invisible: the machine's durableGeneration lags the pool and D6's existing staleness ceiling surfaces it as sync-lag.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:136:- **No self-reinforcing loop:** nothing in boot, applier, or restart paths writes overlay records — writes originate only from the authenticated route funnel. Restart → boot → read is read-only.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:140:**Write authority model (explicit):** `topicId` is REQUIRED on POST and DELETE. The principal resolves through the existing `TopicOperatorStore` surface: each topic carries at most ONE verified operator, bound automatically from the authenticated sender of an authorized message (never from content names). No binding on the named topic ⇒ 400. **Stated as an accepted capability, not merely residual harm:** a Bearer holder CAN write non-suppression keys against any topic that has a verified operator — the Bearer token is the agent's own full-access credential, and anyone holding it already commands the entire API; this surface adds attribution + caps + visibility to a capability the token already implies. For suppression-class keys the same attempt routes the confirm prompt to that topic's verified operator (who did not ask, and declines). A stale binding is the operator's existing topic-operator surface to correct; replayed topicIds change nothing (each write re-resolves the CURRENT binding live).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:165:Because a running machine persists received records immediately (D4), "behind and unaware" cannot exist: every machine is sync-lagged (visible via durableGeneration), apply-lagged (visible via overlayPending), or genuinely diverged (episode).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:170:- **A misbehaving agent session cannot silently suppress detectors:** the suppression class requires the server-matched operator confirm (no Bearer path to commit), and the commit notice is delivered (not recallable). What a misbehaving session CAN do without fresh confirmation is write NON-suppression noise keys (thresholds, intervals, calm levers). Residual harm: bounded to loudness/quietness within clamps, durably attributed, census-visible, tombstone-reversible, restart-capped, churn-capped (6/key/24h). **This is the argued v1 exemption** (formalized in frontmatter): for these keys the API-layer human-confirm would add friction disproportionate to the harm class, and the operator explicitly approved auto-apply for exactly this class.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:171:- **Bearer-token theft** ⇒ the thief can write non-suppression noise knobs (attributed, visible, capped) and can READ the census catalog. The catalog grants no capability beyond what Bearer access already grants everywhere else; hiding the suppression-class column would be obscurity, not structure (declined — the structural controls are the confirm gate + non-recallable notice).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:192:- **The base config file** — machine-local BY DESIGN, pre-existing and unchanged by this spec; `machine-local-justification: physical-credential-locality` (it carries ports/paths/per-machine service bindings and secret refs that physically belong to one disk; the overlay exists precisely so operator noise-intent stops living there). Machine-local operational levers (`operatorSettings.autoRestartToApply`, governance constants) are keys INSIDE this already-declared file — not independent state surfaces. Drift in these levers is not invisible for lack of a posture marker: each machine's census row and `GET /guards` report the lever's LIVE per-machine value, so a machine whose auto-apply is off (or whose governance was tuned) is a read, not a guess.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:193:- **User-facing notices** — the suppression-class notice + pending-stale line ride the EXISTING attention hub with the deterministic dedupe keys above, raised by the machine that commits/holds the state (no pool-wide double-raise). **Standard-B scope note:** these are signal-only disclosures of a deliberate operator decision and a visibility ceiling on a level state — not first-detection escalations of a recoverable degradation, so no self-heal step applies (there is nothing to heal; the "degradation" is the operator's own choice / a visible pending state). D6 rides the existing one-raiser-elected episode flow.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:199:2. **Initial allowlist = the 17 keys in D2**, rejections recorded (incl. both topic-id keys), suppression-class column shipped as store metadata AND census-visible.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:214:17. **Confirm-reply grammar (closed, deterministic):** reply-to-prompt bare `yes`/`no`, or `yes <pendingId>`/`no <pendingId>`; case-insensitive token match; anything else matches nothing (ONE format reminder per pending, then silence). First match wins; edits never re-processed. One open pending per (topic, keyPath); max 3 open per topic; pending ATTEMPTS count toward the per-key churn cap. Principal captured at POST, re-verified at reply; binding change voids the pending.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:215:18. **Post-expiry visibility:** expiry/decline/void sends ONE outcome line to the initiating topic; the pending stays census-visible with status for 24h; immediate re-request allowed (subject to caps).
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:223:- Journal/store sync dark or wedged → machines keep file values (or last-synced overlay); census shows generation lag; nothing blocks.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:225:- Malformed/hostile replicated record → whole-record quarantine, counted, quarantine-visible; quarantined records never count toward generation.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:227:- Pending suppression-confirm expires (operator silent 15 min), is declined, or is voided by an operator-binding change → nothing commits; ONE outcome line to the initiating topic; the pending stays census-visible with its status for 24h. A not-landed change is never silent.
docs/specs/quiet-settings-overlay.md:248:**Migration Parity (P3), stated plainly: every agent-installed change in this spec reaches EXISTING agents through `PostUpdateMigrator` on their next update — nothing here is init-only.** Concretely: config defaults land via `migrateConfig()`; the briefing section lands via `migrateClaudeMd()` (content-sniffing guard) plus the framework-shadow propagation; both are idempotent.
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:28:A background build / autonomous session edits files in its worktree, says "standing by for tests," and then **yields** — and because a `claude -p`-style session ends the moment the model stops emitting, it dies with the work **uncommitted and unpushed**. Real work, zero delivery, invisible for hours (the 2026-06-12 topic-22367 incident).
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:50:**Bounded + fail-open:** the dirty-check runs under a hard **5s** timeout (`SafeGitExecutor.readSync`, which kills the subprocess on expiry); on timeout, git error, missing/unresolvable worktree, or a non-git path the signal is simply **absent** (never a spurious revive, never a wedge). The worktree path is `realpath`-normalized before the check to defeat symlink escapes (and a normalized path that still begins with `-` is rejected so it can't become a git option); `realpathSync`'s built-in loop detection bounds a circular symlink (it throws `ELOOP` rather than hanging → fail-open). So no part of the dirty-check — resolution or git — can hang the killer loop unbounded. The helper's contract is exactly this: never wedge, never spuriously revive. A worktree that *consistently* times out is already separately visible to the operator via #1113's own scan and the reap-log, so the helper does not need its own escalation surface; it simply fail-opens, which is the safe direction.
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:64:3. **The die-again safety net is the dev-live OrphanedWorkSentinel (#1113), not a duplicate in the drainer.** If the revived session **dies again** before resolving, its now-stranded dirty worktree is detected, surfaced (ONE deduped attention item), and — with `preserveWork` on — non-destructively preserved by the **already-shipped, dev-gated `OrphanedWorkSentinel`** (#1113), which runs live on the dev agent alongside this feature and ALREADY scans agent worktrees for exactly this. Re-implementing a second scanner / preserver / delivery-poller inside the drainer would **duplicate** #1113 and violate this spec's own "extends, never duplicates" principle, so it is deliberately NOT done. **Honest scope correction (found at implementation):** the earlier draft's "a worktree that goes clean with zero new commits → preserve the work first" is **not achievable** — by the time any poll/sweep observes "clean, no commit," the work has ALREADY been `reset`/`stash`/discarded and there is nothing left to preserve (catching it would require continuously watching every revived worktree, which neither a drain-tick poll nor a periodic sweep does). The real, honest safety net is therefore: the **directive** drives the commit, the **durable beacon** re-surfaces a stall, and **#1113** detects + preserves + surfaces the *die-again* (post-death, which IS preservable). All of #1113's preservation safety (size caps, secret scrub, path-escape, one-item-per-write) is inherited unchanged — it lives in #1113, not re-built here.
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:84:- **Observability:** the new evidence value + the revival commitment appear in the existing `GET /sessions/resume-queue` and `GET /commitments`; the preservation patches are listed under the OrphanedWorkSentinel surface. No new bespoke route required. A dirty-check that **times out** is logged at WARN (not silently dropped) — so a worktree large/slow enough to consistently time out the 5s check, which would silently disable the feature for that session, is visible to the operator rather than invisible.
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:88:These are resolved here with defaults so the building agent never stops mid-run; the feature ships **dark + dev-gated** (enabled on dev, dark on fleet, per the **Maturation Path** standard) and the tuning knobs below are config-overridable (cheap-to-change), EXCEPT the two marked **durable** (a user-visible/identity surface — deliberately fixed now, contested-and-accepted as non-cheap):
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:100:- **Revival directive (durable):** the verbatim first-line text in §2 R2.1 (agents adapt to its exact phrasing/placement — a user-visible contract, fixed now). When `build-or-autonomous-active` is also present, append this exact second sentence: *"A build/autonomous run was also active when the prior session ended — after resolving the worktree, check on or restart that work too."*
docs/specs/BUILD-SESSION-YIELD-SAFETY-SPEC.md:117:- **Integration:** a pressure reap with a real dirty temp worktree enqueues with the evidence; revival registers ONE beacon-enabled commitment (visible in `/commitments`); a second revival of the same worktree refreshes that row, does NOT open a second; `/sessions/resume-queue` shows the entry; a moved-topic revival on a machine lacking the worktree invalidates with `worktree-not-on-this-machine`, does NOT act, and (holder offline) writes the `cross-machine-invalidations` record.
docs/specs/THREADLINE-SPEC.md:26:| **Invisible by default, observable on demand** | Users should not be interrupted by routine agent chatter, but should always be able to see what's happening. |
docs/specs/THREADLINE-SPEC.md:260:Inter-agent communication should be invisible to the user by default at higher trust levels, but fully observable on demand at any level. The user's autonomy profile gates the level of involvement.
