docs/specs/fable5-quota-dashboard.eli16.md:23:- **A `fable` window on the quota snapshot** — parsed from the usage response's `limits` list. Same shape as the 5-hour and weekly windows, so nothing downstream needs special handling.
docs/specs/fable5-quota-dashboard.eli16.md:24:- **One new dashboard bar** — drawn only when a Fable reading exists; otherwise nothing changes.
docs/specs/fable5-quota-dashboard.eli16.md:28:- **It's read-only.** This is a display of a number, nothing more. It does not gate, block, filter, or decide anything. It is deliberately NOT wired into the model-escalation or scheduling decisions (those keep using the overall limits only) — feeding Fable into a *decision* would be a different, riskier change.
docs/specs/fable5-quota-dashboard.eli16.md:29:- **Multi-machine safety.** The strict cross-machine validator would have rejected any peer's whole snapshot the moment it carried the new `fable` field. We taught it to accept and shape-check `fable` (a real number and a real date, nothing else), so a peer's Fable usage survives sharing instead of nuking that peer's quota on the receiver.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:4:Even excluding the six acknowledged open items, the egress door has three independent unchecked-send classes: conflicting parameters, URL fragments, and URL spellings normalized by `fetch` but missed by the regex. The boundary lint also misses direct Bot API calls already present in `src/`, and several tests/comments/spec claims remain materially false.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:11:1. **DESIGN — Parameter collection has different duplicate and precedence semantics from Telegram.** Query values are collected first, then overwritten by JSON, form, or `URLSearchParams` body values in a single object at [telegram-egress.ts:83](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:83), [telegram-egress.ts:89](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:89), [telegram-egress.ts:99](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:99), [telegram-egress.ts:107](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:107), and [telegram-egress.ts:115](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:115). Telegram’s server appends URL arguments before body arguments, while `Query::arg` returns the first matching argument; duplicate JSON keys are likewise retained in source order by its parser ([HttpReader.cpp](https://github.com/tdlib/td/blob/master/tdnet/td/net/HttpReader.cpp), [Query.h](https://github.com/tdlib/telegram-bot-api/blob/master/telegram-bot-api/Query.h)). Failure path: conflicting reader-visible values make the door inspect a later visible value while Telegram selects an earlier invisible value and sends it.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:13:2. **DESIGN — URL fragments are counted as payload content even though they never go on the wire.** The collector takes every byte after the first `?`, including `#` and the fragment, at [telegram-egress.ts:87](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:87)-[89](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:89). Failure path: an invisible query value followed by visible fragment characters passes `hasNoVisibleCharacters`; `fetch` removes the fragment, so Telegram receives only the invisible value.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:15:3. **DESIGN — Method recognition still compares raw URL text, not the URL that `fetch` dispatches.** `BOT_API_URL` requires the literal host to be followed immediately by `/bot` at [telegram-egress.ts:47](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:47). Valid equivalent spellings such as an explicit default HTTPS port or leading URL whitespace are normalized by `fetch`, but return `null` at [telegram-egress.ts:65](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:65)-[68](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:68). `telegramFetch` then skips every check and calls the network at [telegram-egress.ts:134](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:134)-[159](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:159). Failure path: an invisible reader-visible body sent through such an equivalent URL leaves unchecked.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:17:4. **DESIGN — The lint’s single-door proof still misses ordinary direct-fetch shapes.** `denotesBotApiUrl` resolves only a bare identifier or a directly-called helper at [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:74](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:74)-[78](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:78), and its host marker is case-sensitive at [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:51](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:51), [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:72](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:72), and [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:115](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:115). The live tree already directly fetches Bot API URLs formed as a helper call plus a query suffix at [test-as-self.ts:120](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/commands/test-as-self.ts:120) and [test-as-self.ts:138](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/commands/test-as-self.ts:138), yet the lint prints “confined.” Those calls are currently bodyless reads, but the identical expression shape with a reader-visible method bypasses both the door and lint. Thus the proof and clean output at [lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:219](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:219)-[222](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/scripts/lint-telegram-egress-boundary.mjs:222) are false.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:19:5. **DESIGN — Multipart is over-refused on a false “cannot read without consuming” premise.** `FormData`, `Blob`, and `ArrayBuffer` are grouped with one-shot streams and categorically rejected at [telegram-egress.ts:119](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:119)-[122](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:122); the test repeats the premise at [telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:92](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:92)-[101](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-egress-boundary.test.ts:101). Native `FormData` is iterable without consuming it, while `Blob` and `ArrayBuffer` are repeatably readable. Failure path: a legitimate multipart `sendMessage` carrying visible text is rejected before the network, despite being checkable.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:21:6. **DESIGN — The new door refusals are absent from the promised structured decision stream.** Unknown methods and unreadable bodies throw bare `TelegramEgressError` at [telegram-egress.ts:140](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:140)-[146](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:146) and [telegram-egress.ts:149](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:149)-[154](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/telegram-egress.ts:154), without invoking the sink used at [invisible-payload.ts:373](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:373). That contradicts “every refusal” in the tests at [telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:352](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:352)-[360](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:360) and acceptance criterion 7 at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:456](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:456)-[458](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:458). Failure path: an unreadable invisible request is safely blocked, but produces no structured refusal record, making that refusal class unobservable.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:23:7. **PRECISION — The predicate correction remains only partial.** Runtime includes marks at [invisible-payload.ts:64](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:64), and fixtures accept lone `Mn`, `Mc`, and `Me` values at [telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:297](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:297)-[305](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/tests/unit/telegram-send-funnel-invisible-payload.test.ts:305). The governing definition still says only L/N/P/S and explicitly says a lone mark does not pass at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:225](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:225)-[237](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:237), repeats L/N/P/S at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:247](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:247)-[250](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:250), and omits `M` from its Unicode-drift account at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:318](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:318)-[322](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:322). The route commentary also still describes the deleted subtractive predicate at [routes.ts:14924](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/server/routes.ts:14924)-[14937](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/server/routes.ts:14937). Failure path: a maintainer derives the wrong accepted categories and cross-engine drift surface from the governing prose.
docs/specs/reports/window10-external-passes/pass37-verdict.md:25:8. **PRECISION — Stale architecture and tracking claims remain after the occurrence-local corrections.** The Lifeline still says the deleted predecessor lint enforces its funnel at [TelegramLifeline.ts:2903](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2903)-[2908](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/lifeline/TelegramLifeline.ts:2908); the adapter still claims a `fetch` is called “once, below” at [TelegramAdapter.ts:5754](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:5754)-[5757](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/TelegramAdapter.ts:5757); and the spec says convergence remains pending despite its timestamped convergence tag at [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:13](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:13)-[45](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:45). The vendored-table work is also still assigned to already-landed CMT-1246 at [invisible-payload.ts:85](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:85)-[89](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/src/messaging/invisible-payload.ts:89) and [telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:246](/Users/justin_instar_1/.instar/agents/echo/.worktrees/guard-effectiveness-observability/docs/specs/telegram-egress-invisible-payload-guard.md:246), not CMT-1261. Failure path: future work trusts a nonexistent lint layer or an already-landed commitment as the owner 