docs/specs/FAILURE-LEARNING-INGESTION-SOURCES-SPEC.eli16.md:5:We built a system that's supposed to watch for problems in how I build things and learn from them — but right now it can only "see" a problem if I personally stop and tell it about one. So it's wide awake and staring at nothing. The fancy Process Health dashboard tab will keep saying "all quiet" forever, not because everything's perfect, but because nobody's feeding it.
docs/specs/test-runner-concurrency-bound.eli16.md:5:When our test suite runs, it doesn't run as one process — it spins up a worker for roughly every CPU core on the machine. That's fine when one test suite runs at a time. But on a development machine where several AI agent sessions each build and test their own changes, nothing stops many suites from starting at once. On 2026-07-02, twenty-nine test suites ran simultaneously on one machine: roughly 300 worker processes fought for 16 cores, the agent servers sharing the machine froze, and the health watchdogs — misreading the freeze as sickness — killed healthy servers. The safety system became the outage.
docs/specs/test-runner-concurrency-bound.eli16.md:17:A test suite that hangs forever would jam the lane forever, so after a generous ceiling (60 minutes, tunable) the system reclaims the stuck suite's ticket so the lane keeps moving. That timer is bounded at BOTH ends: review caught that a ceiling alone wasn't enough — a stray setting could have set the timer to *near-zero*, which would have quietly switched the whole limit off (and, in the opt-in force-stop mode, interrupted healthy runs), so the timer is now clamped to a sane range (never below 5 minutes, never above 4 hours) and a garbage value falls back to the safe default with a loud note. A related simplification this round: nested test runs (a test that itself launches tests) used to have their own intricate bookkeeping; review from two outside models agreed that machinery was the biggest source of complexity and risk for a rare case, so nested runs are now simply kept small (a modest worker count) with no bookkeeping at all — less code, fewer ways to go wrong, and the worst-case machine load drops as a bonus. The very next review round then caught that this simplification had gone one step too far: a nested run could carry a command-line flag that overrode the "keep it small" limit, and with the old bookkeeping gone nothing else held it back. So the fix makes that limit command-line-proof (the flag is stripped before the test runner sees it), keeping the simpler design while closing the hole — a good example of the review process catching a regression the same round it was introduced. **By default it does NOT kill anything** — it just takes the ticket back and writes a loud note in the log. A semaphore's job is to free up capacity, not to execute processes, and review made clear that killing by remembered process-ID is genuinely risky: process IDs get recycled, so the number written down an hour ago might now belong to something innocent (even another agent's server). So force-stopping a hung suite is a *separate, opt-in* mode that stays off through the whole trial period and only an operator turns on later — and it can only be turned on in one place (a single settings file on the machine); a stray environment variable inherited from some shell can turn it OFF but is structurally incapable of turning it ON. Even then it is fenced four ways before any signal: the target ID must be sane, its start-time and command line must still match the recorded test run (defeating ID recycling), it must be its own process-group leader before a group-wide signal is allowed (so a shared group — a git-push hook, a terminal pane — is never caught in the blast), and the "if it ignores the polite stop, escalate to a hard stop" step is written down durably so it completes even if the process that noticed the hang exits first. If any check fails, the ticket is freed but nothing is touched.
docs/specs/test-runner-concurrency-bound.eli16.md:22:2. **Watch-only first.** The system ships observing: it takes tickets, counts, and writes a durable log of every "I would have blocked this" decision — but blocks nothing and signals nothing. Only after a bounded soak (at least 14 days and 50 real suite runs with zero false would-blocks, and a check that waiting pushes weren't being starved) does the owner flip one file on the machine to turn enforcement on. The flip is tracked as a commitment so "watch-only" can't quietly become "forever"; if the window lapses with no decision, the guard dashboard flags it loudly.
docs/specs/test-runner-concurrency-bound.eli16.md:26:Full suites serialize: on a busy multi-agent machine, someone's push waits for someone else's suite. That's the deliberate price of the operator's rule, and the log makes the cost measurable so the cap can be raised with evidence. And because the system deliberately errs toward letting runs through when its own bookkeeping is broken, it's honestly a *best-effort* bound — in a rare stuck-lock situation a few extra runs can slip through for a short window (the safe direction), and those windows are logged and visible rather than silent. Even that failure mode has a hard floor, though: if a broken lock ever let runs pile up toward storm levels, a separate counter that doesn't depend on the lock stops admitting more — "let it through when unsure" flips off at exactly the point where letting more through would BE the meltdown. Older work branches that predate the change don't carry the ticket-taking code until they rebase — the bound covers the machine progressively, not instantly. And the whole thing is per-machine, per-user by design (a test run burns THIS machine's cores; there's nothing to coordinate across machines).
