docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:90:What we add: nothing in the beacon itself. A threadline-await commitment is just another commitment, so the beacon handles it out of the box. Optionally we can register a tiny `classifyProgress` callback that asks "is this thread still alive on the other side?" but that's an optimization, not a requirement.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:99:What we add: nothing required. For genuinely multi-agent jobs (e.g., the Stripe ask fanning out to ai-guy + Dawn + a backup direct-API pull), users can manually create an Initiative and link the threadline-await commitments to its phases. Surfacing an Initiative for every single threadline await would be over-engineering. Reserve for multi-step work.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:203:        if salience == 'user-visible':
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:234:**Output:** `'user-visible' | 'agent-internal'` plus a one-line reason.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:240:> Mark `user-visible` if the reply:
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:252:**Failure mode:** If the gate errors or times out (>2s), default to `user-visible` for the first reply on a thread (no `lastReplyAt` recorded yet) and `agent-internal` for subsequent replies. Bias toward not flooding the user but not silently swallowing first contact.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:258:When salience is `user-visible`, the router posts to the originating topic via `TelegramAdapter`. Format:
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:270:Rate-limit: max 1 user-visible surface per thread per minute, batched if multiple arrive in the window. (Defends against a chatty reply burst.)
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:290:Salience: {user-visible | agent-internal}
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:291:{If user-visible: A Telegram notification was already sent to topic {topicId}.}
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:330:If injection fails and the session can't be resumed (memory pressure, no available process slot), the reply is queued in the existing inbound queue *and* the user gets a notification regardless of salience: "Reply arrived from {agent} on '{subject}' but I couldn't pick it up automatically — let me know when to retry." The commitment stays `pending` (not transitioned to `delivered`) so PromiseBeacon continues to heartbeat — the user has visible follow-through that something is wedged.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:390:- Router decision tree: 6 cases — has-origin-and-live, has-origin-and-dormant, has-origin-but-topic-expired, no-origin, salience-user-visible, salience-agent-internal.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:394:- End-to-end: topic session sends → server stamps origin + creates commitment → PromiseBeacon registers the commitment → simulated reply arrives → topic session is resumed → commitment transitions to `delivered` → beacon stops heartbeating → Telegram notification posts when salience is user-visible.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:397:- Spawn-denied failure path: inject mock failure, verify commitment stays pending and user gets failure-visible notification.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:406:4. Confirm PromiseBeacon picked it up (visible in commitments list).
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:422:2. **User-visible flooding.** If the salience gate over-triggers user-visible, the user gets spammed. Mitigation: per-thread rate-limit per §5.5.
docs/specs/THREAD-TOPIC-LINKAGE-SPEC.md:444:13. **LLM-backed salience classifier.** The `SalienceGate` accepts a `classify` callback via its constructor but v1 wires no classifier — the deterministic fallback (user-visible on first reply per thread, agent-internal subsequently) is what ships. A Haiku-class classifier with the §5.4 rubric should be wired in v2 so the user-visibility decision becomes content-aware rather than recency-based.
docs/specs/auto-updater-restart-activation-visibility.eli16.md:9:The change also makes the restart wait visible. AutoUpdater now persists a restart-wait object in its state file with the target version, when the wait first started, why activation is waiting, the current blockers, and the next retry time. Authenticated health and update status expose the same object, so "installed but not active" is no longer hidden in logs. The misleading "binary resolution mismatch" wording is replaced for intentional restart waits: the log now says the new version is installed but restart activation is intentionally waiting.
