docs/specs/WORKING-SET-HANDOFF-SPEC.eli16.md:5:How it works, plainly: each machine can compute, on demand, "what files make up this conversation's workspace" — from durable evidence (the run's own file, anything matching the topic's file-naming convention, and every path the diary recorded), never from anyone remembering to declare anything. When a conversation moves, the receiving machine asks the machines that actually produced the work — not just the last owner, who may have produced nothing — "send me that workspace." Files travel in small one-megabyte slices (the review caught that my draft's single 32MB response would have re-created this laptop's famous freeze-under-load incident), each slice fingerprint-checked, the whole file verified before a single byte lands in place.
docs/specs/WORKING-SET-HANDOFF-SPEC.eli16.md:12:- **Nothing is ever overwritten, and nothing can be deleted.** A genuinely different local version gets the incoming copy saved ALONGSIDE it (capped at two, identical arrivals collapse to one) plus one calm notice — real divergence gets seen, not coin-flipped.
docs/specs/WORKING-SET-HANDOFF-SPEC.eli16.md:32:**Third live-proof amendment (2026-06-06, finding #5):** the third run caught the deepest half of the same gap — both earlier fixes taught the receiving machine to READ ownership evidence, but moving a QUIET conversation (one with no recent messages) never WROTE any: the router pinned it privately and the diary stayed silent, so there was still nothing to read. Fixed at the source: the move itself now writes the ownership record properly — and confirms it in the same breath, so the conversation never rests in a half-placed state where later messages would silently queue — and that diary entry travels to the receiving machine like every other. Three safety rails: a conversation another machine is actively running is never taken from it; a half-placed leftover from before the fix is repaired on the next move; anything in-flight is left strictly alone.
