docs/specs/reports/DOORWAY-MODEL-KNOWLEDGE-REGISTRY-SPEC-convergence.md:9:We keep a map of *which doors we can walk through to reach an AI model* (the Claude app, the Codex tool, pi, a paid Google/OpenRouter key, Antigravity) and *what the best model behind each door is*. That map quietly went stale once — some of our helpers used a year-old Google model for weeks because our list said it was best and nothing ever re-checked. This spec builds three things so that can't happen silently again: (1) a proper, growing **registry** of doors + top models (with exact ids + prices), split into a *reviewed* half that ships with instar and a *live per-machine* half of "what this computer could actually reach when it last looked"; (2) a **robot that re-checks on a schedule**, tells us **only what changed**, is careful about money (the normal check is free; anything paid needs a manual switch AND a spending limit or it refuses) and secrets (it only ever handles key *names*), and physically can't edit our real files; (3) a written **standard** that says a stale map is a bug, kept current by machinery, not by someone remembering.
docs/specs/reports/DOORWAY-MODEL-KNOWLEDGE-REGISTRY-SPEC-convergence.md:36:| 7 (confirmation) | **(converged)** | **0** | none — codex external: only documented-tradeoff re-raises; adversarial + integration confirm the round-6 folds opened nothing; conformance: only the LLM-variance dark-gate re-flag (documented exception) |
docs/specs/reports/DOORWAY-MODEL-KNOWLEDGE-REGISTRY-SPEC-convergence.md:48:**Round 6 (this run).** MEDIUM: fresh/lost/new-machine scan-state diffed against an empty baseline → full-map flood, breaking §1.3's "surfaces nothing until it has a baseline" promise (integration) → initial-seed carve-out. MINOR: `undeliveredDiff` replay-vs-recompute ambiguity + unnamed sink (adversarial) → pure `lastDeliveryFailedAt` timestamp marker, retry always recomputes. BUG (codex #1, real): Migration Parity still said `migrateConfig()` seeds `enabled:false`, contradicting the D6 fix → seed every field except `enabled`. Plus the debounce-asymmetry clarification (adversarial, round-5-carried) and codex's supervised-job justification + evidence fields + compensating-control notes.
docs/specs/reports/DOORWAY-MODEL-KNOWLEDGE-REGISTRY-SPEC-convergence.md:60:**Converged.** The material-finding count decayed monotonically across the run (blockers → HIGH → medium/minor → one non-regressing clarification → zero), every finding was folded into the spec, and the final confirmation round produced **zero new material findings**: the codex/GPT external returned only documented-tradeoff re-raises, the two reviewers whose round-6 findings were folded (adversarial, integration) confirmed the fixes resolved and opened nothing new, decision-completeness stayed clean (11 frontloaded decisions, 3 contested-then-cleared cheap tags), and the conformance gate's only recurring flag is a documented, LLM-variance re-flag of an accepted exception. The spec is ready for user review and approval.
docs/specs/reports/build-session-yield-safety-convergence.md:9:A background "build" session does its work in a private copy of the code (a *worktree*) and then stops — but those sessions die the instant they stop typing, so they can vanish with their edits **never saved to git**. The work is on disk but invisible; on 2026-06-12 that lost hours. Two existing safety nets miss this exact case (one only rescues written-down *promises*; the other only *notices* the dead session's files after the fact). This spec adds the missing piece: it makes "unsaved work in a worktree" a real **wake-up signal** that brings the session back, and gives the revived session a **tracked, nagging obligation** to actually save the work — plus a **read-only backup patch** as a last resort if it dies again. It's honestly **loss-reduction, not a guarantee**, ships **off by default** (on the dev agent first), and every failure path falls back to today's behavior, never to a stuck session.
