docs/specs/reports/integrated-being-ledger-v2-convergence.md:15:But v1 was the READ side only. It couldn't help with the *other* half of the problem that surfaced the very next day: sessions making promises nothing could back ("I'll relay Dawn's response") and subsessions making substantive agreements (API contracts, integration designs) that nobody upstream could see.
docs/specs/reports/integrated-being-ledger-v2-convergence.md:17:v2 is the WRITE side. It adds three things. First, a new "commitment" entry type that carries a mechanism (how will this be backed — a job? a scheduled check? nothing?), a deadline, and a status. Second, a sanctioned session-write endpoint with proper authentication — sessions can now say "I am committing to X, here's my mechanism" and it gets recorded in a way other sessions can see. Third, a resolution workflow so commitments can be marked resolved (by the session, by a server-side witness, or by you at the dashboard) with tiered trust levels.
docs/specs/reports/integrated-being-ledger-v2-convergence.md:19:The promise this spec makes: if an agent session says "I'll do X by Y," there's now a durable place for that promise to live, a mechanism for it to be backed, and a way to see when it isn't. The commitments become visible infrastructure rather than decorative words.
docs/specs/reports/integrated-being-ledger-v2-convergence.md:33:5. **Session self-assert is creator-only.** Original allowed any session to self-assert any commitment as resolved — a cross-session hiding attack. Now only the creating session can self-assert; other sessions must use the visible `dispute` path.
docs/specs/reports/integrated-being-ledger-v2-convergence.md:75:Unlike v1 (where cross-model review was noted as not working), v2's convergence included full cross-model review across all three iterations: GPT, Gemini, and Grok via `call-llm.cjs`. All three returned substantive reviews with concrete findings. Gemini caught the bootstrap confused-deputy architectural concern that none of the internal reviewers surfaced — the value of genuinely independent perspectives is visible in the iteration log.
docs/specs/reports/codex-intelligence-provider-clean-call-convergence.md:7:The review process changed one important thing: *which* throwaway folder. The first draft used a single, predictably-named folder in the system temp area. On a shared Linux machine, that predictable name is a security hole — another user could create that folder first and slip a malicious "hook" file into it that Codey would then run as himself. The fix now creates a folder with a random, unguessable name and locked-down (owner-only) permissions, so nothing can be planted in it. The tradeoff is essentially nil: it's the same mechanism, just with a safe folder name.
docs/specs/reports/codex-intelligence-provider-clean-call-convergence.md:12:- **After review:** the folder is created with `fs.mkdtempSync(...)`, which appends a random suffix, sets owner-only (`0700`) permissions, and refuses to reuse a pre-existing path. The path is now unguessable, so nothing can be planted in it. The provider also re-checks the folder before each call and recreates it if a temp-cleaner removed it. The now-unused `workingDirectory` field was removed (kept on the type only for API compatibility).
