docs/specs/reports/_convergence-findings/round1-scalability.md:18:- **[minor]** Memoized effective config can go stale vs. a CLI installed/removed mid-run — but the staleness direction matters — §4.4 memoizes the active-set "once at boot". `detectFrameworkBinary` is process-memoized for the whole lifetime (`Config.ts:146`, caches negative results too), so a provider CLI installed *after* boot is invisible until restart, and the router's per-framework cache (`IntelligenceRouter.ts:143`) also never re-probes. Practically low-impact (operators rarely `npm i -g codex` mid-run), and the spec's "self-heals when the operator installs/removes a provider" claim in §5 is **only true across a restart**, not live. **Resolution:** Correct the §5 wording — it self-heals on next boot, not live — and confirm that's acceptable (it is, given config is the live-read layer and provider availability legitimately changes only at install/restart boundaries). No code change needed; just don't overclaim "live self-heal".
docs/specs/reports/_convergence-findings/round1-security.md:9:- **[material]** Making Codex the default provider for ALL gating prompts materially widens the prompt-content exposure surface, and the spec's safety analysis (§6) does not address it. Every gating call (MessagingToneGate, MessageSentinel, ExternalOperationGate, InputGuard, IntentLlmJudge, RelationshipAnomalyScorer, LlmIntentClassifier) sends its FULL prompt — which routinely contains the user's outbound/inbound message text, relationship/PII context, and operation details — to a different vendor's CLI child process by default, for agents that today send none of it there. The exec-json path is hardened (prompt via stdin, scratch-dir hygiene, env allowlist via `buildCodexChildEnv`), but the kill-switch `evaluatePlain` path passes the prompt as a ps-visible positional argv and embeds it in thrown `error.message` (its own SECURITY NOTE). Flipping a default that routes safety-gate content to codex by default + the legacy-path disclosure channel is a real data-residency/exposure change the operator should consciously accept. — **Resolution:** Add an explicit "data-exposure posture" subsection to §6 stating that gating-prompt content (incl. message text + relationship PII) now reaches the Codex/Gemini provider by default, that the default exec-json path keeps the prompt off argv/stderr, and that the `codexExecJson:false` kill-switch re-opens the ps-visible disclosure channel. State that gemini-cli (likewise default-chain) is acceptable for this content class, or scope PII-bearing gates (RelationshipAnomalyScorer) out of the off-Claude default if not.
docs/specs/reports/failure-learning-loop-convergence.md:15:- **The "easy first step" was wrong.** v1 claimed we could catch failures for free by reusing a signal the system already tracks. Review (4 reviewers, code-grounded) found that signal actually means "a release switch was flipped backward" or "a commit was rebased away" — not "a shipped feature broke." The converged spec makes the honest first step a fix-commit that names the feature it repairs, plus a one-tap diagnosis, and fixes the test that would otherwise have passed while proving nothing.
