docs/specs/reports/_convergence-findings/round1-decision-completeness.md:11:- **[material] Q1 — Active-provider probe: `buildProvider` truth (a) vs. `which <cli>` (b)?** → **Type B (engineering).** Reversible, internal, no external side-effect, no user-visible interface. The spec already leans (a) with sound justification (single source of truth — the active-set must match exactly what the router's `resolveProvider`/`buildProvider` will actually return at call time; a second `which`-based notion of "available" can drift and route to a framework that then degrades). The agent can and should decide this. **Resolution (in-spec):** use (a) — call `buildProvider(fw) !== null` once per framework at boot to compute the active set, reusing the SAME `buildProvider` closure already wired at `server.ts:4699`. This is strictly more honest than `which`: it captures pi-cli's TWO-precondition reality (binary present AND a `frameworkDefaultModels['pi-cli']` model pattern configured — see `intelligenceProviderFactory.ts` pi-cli case, which returns null without the model pattern). A `which pi` probe would falsely mark pi "active", make it the primary, and then degrade every call. (a) cannot make that mistake. Memoize the result at boot; the live-read of operator config is preserved separately (§4.4). NOT a user decision.
docs/specs/reports/_convergence-findings/round1-decision-completeness.md:13:- **[material] Q2 — Runtime-computed default vs. one-time migrated config block?** → **Type B (engineering).** Internal, reversible, no durable external side-effect that a user must own. The spec leans runtime-computed with the correct reasoning (self-heals when the operator later installs/removes a provider CLI; a persisted block pins a stale active-set and re-introduces a migration-parity hazard). **Resolution (in-spec):** runtime-computed, NO persisted block. `migrateConfig()` writes nothing for this feature (leave `componentFrameworks` unset so the resolver stays live). The "migration" is purely the new code shipping — zero migration-parity risk, always reflects the currently-installed providers. This also keeps the operator-override precedence (§4.3) trivially correct: "unset" is an unambiguous signal to apply the computed default; a migrated block would blur "operator set this" vs. "we wrote this," breaking the §4.3 verbatim-passthrough rule. NOT a user decision.
docs/specs/reports/_convergence-findings/round1-decision-completeness.md:38:- **Cheap-to-change / "ships behind a default" tags contested:** Q4 contested (the "may surprise" framing) — but the contest CLEARS to Type B, because the surface is internal, reversible, operator-overridable, and floored by the §4.2 no-op. NO durable external side-effect, money, identity, or user-visible published interface is touched by ANY of Q1/Q2/Q4/Q5 — the one user-visible surface (`GET /intelligence/routing` output + the CLAUDE.md template note) is additive/observability, not a contract change. So no "cheap-to-change-after" claim survives that needed escalation to Type A.
