     1	# Making sure a message you can't see never gets sent — in plain terms
     2	
     3	## What actually went wrong once
     4	
     5	Someone's agent sent a message whose entire contents were a single invisible character — the kind that
     6	takes up no space and shows nothing on screen. The send failed in a confusing way, retried nine times over
     7	more than four hours, and finally told the user: *"I had a reply for you but couldn't deliver it."*
     8	
     9	There was no reply. There never had been. The user spent that whole time believing something had been lost.
    10	
    11	So we added a check: if a message contains nothing a person could actually see, refuse to send it and say
    12	so plainly, rather than delivering emptiness or inventing a delivery failure.
    13	
    14	## The part that kept going wrong
    15	
    16	The check itself has always worked. **What kept being wrong was our belief about where it needed to be.**
    17	
    18	Four separate times, someone put the check somewhere, wrote down "that's all of them", and was proved wrong
    19	by the next person who looked:
    20	
    21	1. Put it on one entry point → *"fixed at the point of sending."* Someone found a second entry point.
    22	2. Put it on both → *"both doors."* Someone found a third.
    23	3. Moved it to what looked like the one place everything funnels through → *"the single chokepoint every
    24	   send passes through."* Someone ran the code and found a way straight past it.
    25	4. Moved it to the function that actually talks to the network → *"the one function everything reaches."*
    26	   A reviewer found a branch that skips that function entirely.
    27	
    28	That last one was mine, today, in the very change whose job was to fix the pattern.
    29	
    30	**The habit underneath is the same every time: saying "that's the whole set" without going and counting.**
    31	
    32	## What this change does
    33	
    34	**It counts, and it puts the check at every exit — not at whichever function looks central.**
    35	
    36	Counting properly turned up **six** places in our code that can send a Telegram message, not the two
    37	everyone had been talking about. One of them — the piece that keeps the agent reachable when it's running
    38	as a backup on another machine — had **no check at all**, and had been missed by all four previous counts
    39	for one simple reason: every count only looked at the main messaging file.
    40	
    41	It also turned out one file has **two different ways out**, not one. Normally it talks to Telegram directly.
    42	But when it's a backup machine without its own credentials, it hands the message to another machine to send
    43	instead — and that path never touches the function everyone assumed was the bottleneck.
    44	
    45	So the rule changed from *"guard the funnel"* to **"guard every exit."**
    46	
    47	## Two smaller things worth knowing
    48	
    49	**A topic's name counts as something you can see.** Chat topics have titles, and it was possible to create
    50	one whose title was entirely invisible — it passes the "is this at least one character long?" test, because
    51	those invisible marks *are* characters. That leaves a permanently unfindable topic sitting in the list.
    52	Now names are checked the same way message bodies are.
    53	
    54	**Not everything with text in it is a message.** The little grey confirmation that pops up when you tap a
    55	button is allowed to be empty — that's how you dismiss it. Refusing those would be the check overreaching,
    56	so they're deliberately left alone, and there's a test that fails if someone widens it later.
    57	
    58	## Then the check itself turned out to be wrong, twice
    59	
    60	An outside reviewer went at this fourteen times, and twice it found that the check was **letting real things
    61	through** — not a wording problem, an actual hole, each one confirmed by running the code.
    62	
    63	**The first: the check was written backwards.** It listed the invisible things and removed them, then treated
    64	whatever was left as visible. That sounds fine until you ask what "whatever is left" contains. It contained
    65	control characters, code points nobody has assigned a meaning to yet, private-use characters, half of a
    66	character pair, and accent marks with no letter to sit on. **All of those passed as "visible" and would have
    67	been sent.** Every one shows a reader nothing — the same harm as the original incident, hiding inside the fix
    68	for the original incident.
    69	
    70	You can only remove the shapes you thought of. So the check was turned around to name what **counts**
    71	instead: a letter, a number, a punctuation mark, or a symbol. Anything else is not content — including
    72	whatever gets invented in a future version of Unicode.
    73	
    74	**The second: even that had blind spots.** A Hangul filler is officially a letter. A blank Braille cell is
    75	officially a symbol. Both render as empty space. Five characters like that sailed through the new check.
    76	They are now excluded by name, with tests pinning each one — and the remaining risk is stated plainly rather
    77	than hidden: some future character could be officially a letter and still look like nothing, and that would
    78	pass until someone adds it.
    79	
    80	## The safeguard against this happening a fifth time
    81	
    82	Rather than trusting the next person to remember, there's now an automatic check that **works out the list
    83	of senders for itself** — anything in the code that contacts Telegram and sends words — and fails the build
    84	if any of them is missing the guard.
    85	
    86	An independent reviewer then tried to defeat that automatic check, and **succeeded five different ways**,
    87	including simply commenting the guard out (the checker still reported everything fine) and hiding one
    88	sender from the count (it cheerfully reported "all clear — 5 senders" while quietly no longer looking at
    89	the sixth). All five holes are closed, and the checker now also refuses to accept the list getting
    90	*shorter* without someone explicitly saying why.
    91	
    92	## One more thing the review insisted on
    93	
    94	A check that blocks things is supposed to keep a record of what it blocked and why — otherwise, when it
    95	refuses something it shouldn't have, nobody can find out. This one kept no record at all. That was pointed
    96	out, not acted on, and pointed out again nine rounds later. It now writes down which operation, which field,
    97	which rule, how long the payload was, and which version of the software decided — and never the payload
    98	itself, because an invisible message is still someone's content.
    99	
   100	## What you'd notice
   101	
   102	Almost certainly nothing, and that's the intent. Real messages send exactly as before — including a message
   103	that's just a full stop, which is visible and therefore fine.
   104	
   105	The one behaviour change: something trying to send you a genuinely empty message now fails immediately and
   106	says *why*, instead of either delivering nothing or telling you your reply got lost.
   107	
   108	## What this does not cover, said plainly
   109	
   110	- The automatic check confirms each sender *has* the guard; it can't prove the guard sits on the exact line
   111	  the message travels. Tests cover that instead.
   112	- If someone invents a completely new way to reach Telegram, it won't be in the derived list. The
   113	  shrink-alarm makes a disappearance loud, but it can't predict a shape nobody has written yet.
   114	- This is Telegram only. Slack and the others are untouched, and nothing here claims otherwise.
   115	
   116	## What was decided, and what is still owed
   117	
   118	**Approved on 2026-08-10.** The work is built, tested, independently reviewed, and taken through fourteen
   119	rounds with an outside reviewer.
   120	
   121	**What is honestly still owed**, because it should not be buried: three of the send paths are proven by
   122	actually pushing an invisible message through them and watching it be refused. **Four smaller ones — two
   123	setup greetings, a self-test, and a demo tool — are only proven to have the check written into the file**,
   124	not proven by running it. And the whole design leans on a build-time scan of the source code rather than on
   125	there being one single place a message can leave from. That single place is the real fix, it is written
   126	down, it has an owner and a date, and until it exists nobody should describe this as airtight.

