     3	## Cross-model review: codex-cli:gpt-5.5
     4	
     5	RAN. A real GPT-tier external pass ran through the agent's codex CLI on **every one of the 14 rounds** — no
     6	round was degraded, skipped, or unavailable. `crossFamily: true` on all fourteen.
     7	
     8	## ELI10 Overview
     9	
    10	A message whose entire body is invisible — nothing but spaces and zero-width marks — was once delivered to a
    11	user, failed strangely, retried for over four hours, and produced a "your reply couldn't be delivered"
    12	notice for a reply that never existed. A check was added to refuse those.
    13	
    14	The check itself always worked. What kept being wrong was **where we believed it needed to be**. Four times
    15	someone placed it, wrote "that's all of them", and was proved wrong by the next reader. This change is the
    16	fifth placement, and its point is that it stops relying on anyone's belief about the set: the senders are
    17	worked out mechanically from the code, and a build check fails if any of them lacks the guard.
    18	
    19	The review found two live holes in the check itself along the way. It was **subtractive** — remove the
    20	known-invisible things, treat the rest as visible — which let control characters, unassigned code points,
    21	private-use characters, lone combining marks and lone surrogates through, all of which show a reader
    22	nothing. It is positive now: content means a letter, number, punctuation mark or symbol. Then the positive
    23	version turned out to have its *own* blind spot — a Hangul filler is a letter and a Braille blank is a
    24	symbol, and both render as empty space. Both holes were found by an outside reviewer and confirmed by
    25	running the code, not by argument.
    26	
    27	## Original vs Converged
    28	
    29	| | before review | after 14 rounds |
    30	|---|---|---|
    31	| the predicate | subtractive — remove known-invisible, keep the rest | positive — content is a letter, number, punctuation or symbol, minus five known blank glyphs |
    32	| non-printing input | C0 controls, unassigned, private-use, noncharacters, lone marks, lone surrogates ALL DELIVERED | all refused, each pinned by a fixture |
    33	| method classification | open world — in the map = guarded, everything else silently unguarded | closed world — every method declared visible-with-field or explicitly bodyless; anything else FAILS as review-required |
    34	| refusal records | none | structured record per refusal (method, field, rule, length, engine), emitted before the throw, payload never logged |
    35	| the claim | "at every EGRESS" | "at every agent funnel (input-proven), guard-call present in every derived sender file (presence-only)" |
    36	| architecture honesty | presented as the fix | presented as an INTERIM, non-structural guard; CMT-1246 is the boundary |
    37	| signal-vs-authority fit | cited the enumerable-inputs exception | corrected to hard-invariant validation at the API edge — the enumerable-inputs citation was wrong for four rounds |
    38	
    39	## Iteration Summary
    40	
    41	| round | verdict | design-class | precision-class | what moved |
    42	|---|---|---|---|---|
    43	| 1 | MINOR | 3 | 1 | title overclaimed "every EGRESS"; multi-machine `unified` unfounded; method map called timeless |
